/*!
\page phy_acceleration_architecture PHY Acceleration

PHY acceleration is integrated as an optional implementation detail of PHY and
Open Fronthaul processing. The generic modem logic depends on existing OCUDU
interfaces and implementation-neutral resident-buffer contracts. Concrete
accelerator details such as CUDA streams, CUDA-visible buffers and CUDA
kernels stay in backend factories or backend implementation files.

\section phy_acceleration_goals Design Goals

- Keep dependency arrows pointing inward. Generic PUSCH, PDSCH, PRACH, SRS,
  lower-PHY and OFH orchestration code must depend on OCUDU interfaces, not
  concrete accelerator classes.
- Preserve substitutability. Host-only, hardware-accelerated and CUDA-backed
  implementations must satisfy the same public contracts and continue to build
  when CUDA support is disabled.
- Keep real-time paths explicit. Resident buffers avoid unnecessary host-device
  transfers, but ownership and synchronization are represented by small opaque
  tokens rather than leaking backend types into generic call sites.
- Keep factories responsible for selection. Mode parsing, availability checks,
  concrete backend construction and fallback selection belong in factory or
  strategy modules, not in protocol or slot-processing logic.

\section phy_acceleration_configuration Configuration Surface

Acceleration is selected through the existing application configuration model.
The expert PHY, SDR RU and OFH cell configuration structures expose string modes
whose values are \c auto, \c enabled and \c disabled. The \c auto mode lets the
factory choose an accelerated implementation when the build, platform and PDU
shape support it. The \c enabled mode requires the accelerated path and fails
configuration or factory creation when it is unavailable. The \c disabled mode
keeps the host implementation selected.

Upper-PHY controls cover PUSCH, PDSCH, PRACH detection, SRS estimation, PDSCH
acceleration lanes and the accelerated LDPC decoder algorithm. SDR RU controls
cover lower-PHY transmit baseband processing, regular receive OFDM demodulation
and PRACH OFDM demodulation. OFH cell controls cover User-Plane IQ compression
and decompression acceleration.

\section phy_acceleration_layers Layering

The public acceleration boundary is declared in implementation-neutral types.
\ref ocudu::resident_softbit_buffer, \ref ocudu::resident_uci_buffer and
\ref ocudu::pusch_resident_demodulation_config describe resident PUSCH data and
execution dependencies without naming a concrete runtime API.
\ref ocudu::resource_grid_reader, \ref ocudu::resource_grid_writer and
\ref ocudu::prach_buffer expose optional device-capable hooks while preserving
the host resource-grid contract. OFH compression and U-Plane decoder interfaces
expose optional direct grid and PRACH-buffer paths that return false when an
implementation cannot use them.

The generic PUSCH processor consumes the resident PUSCH demodulator and decoder
methods. It does not include CUDA or CUDA headers. PDSCH uses a
\ref ocudu::pdsch_grid_output_strategy to choose how a processed PDSCH writes
into the downlink resource grid. Upper PHY resource-grid selection is isolated in
the PHY acceleration resource-grid factory helper. Lower-PHY transmit and
receive paths select accelerated OFDM and baseband implementations through their
standard factories. OFH compression selection is kept in the compression factory
and the U-Plane message builder/decoder paths.

Concrete CUDA code lives in backend implementation modules, for example
the CUDA LDPC adapter, PUSCH CUDA demodulator, PDSCH CUDA block processor,
PRACH detector, SRS estimator, lower-PHY OFDM demodulators, lower-PHY transmit
modulator, OFH compression adapter and CUDA-visible resource-grid
implementation. These modules may include CUDA headers and use backend-specific
synchronization.

\section phy_acceleration_processing_paths Processing Paths

PUSCH acceleration covers resource-grid reading, channel estimation handoff,
resident demodulation, optional resident UCI compaction and resident LDPC
decoding. Host demodulation and host decode remain available for unsupported
PDU shapes and for CUDA-disabled builds.

PDSCH acceleration covers transport-block encoding, modulation and resource-grid
mapping. The PDSCH processor writes through a grid output strategy so host-grid,
device-grid and sidecar-grid implementations remain interchangeable.

PRACH acceleration has two independent stages: lower-PHY PRACH OFDM
demodulation, which produces PRACH buffer contents, and upper-PHY PRACH
detection, which consumes the buffer. The PRACH buffer interface carries the
optional resident-buffer hooks used to connect those stages.

SRS acceleration is selected at the SRS estimator factory. The generic estimator
interface and caller-visible result types are unchanged.

Lower-PHY acceleration covers transmit baseband modulation, regular PUxCH OFDM
demodulation and PRACH OFDM demodulation. Optional packed-sample methods allow
implementations to consume radio \c ci16 buffers directly when supported.

OFH acceleration covers BFP compression and decompression for host spans,
resource grids, PRACH buffers, contiguous port batches and symbol batches. The
same compressor and decoder interfaces preserve the host fallback path.

\section phy_acceleration_contracts Interface Contracts

Resident PUSCH handoff follows this contract:

- The demodulator may advertise resident mode support.
- When resident mode is enabled, the demodulator may return an accelerator-owned
  softbit buffer and optional compact UCI output.
- The decoder may advertise resident decode support and consume the resident
  softbit buffer without requiring a host softbit copy.
- Opaque execution and completion tokens are valid only for the backend that
  produced them.
- If any implementation cannot honor the resident contract for a PDU, it must
  return false or an invalid buffer and allow the host path to run.

The public resident PUSCH handoff API is expressed in accelerator-owned buffer
terms. Concrete backend details remain in the corresponding implementation
modules.

Device-capable resource-grid and PRACH-buffer hooks follow this contract:

- A capability method advertises whether the optional device path is supported.
- Opaque device pointers and synchronization tokens are valid only for the
  implementation that produced them.
- Prepare, enqueue and synchronize methods are backend handshakes; host-only
  implementations return the default fallback values.
- If an accelerated mapping or read cannot be used for a PDU, the caller must
  continue through the host path.

OFH direct compression and decompression follows this contract:

- Direct grid and PRACH-buffer methods return true only after fully producing
  the requested payload or destination range.
- A false return means the caller should use the ordinary host span path.
- Batch methods use symbol-major or port-major layouts as documented by their
  interface comments.

\section phy_acceleration_runtime Runtime Policy

Production configuration should use the implementation-neutral acceleration mode
fields. Environment variables are reserved for platform policy overrides,
focused profiling or sensitivity experiments. Any new runtime knob must be
either promoted to the configuration schema with operator documentation or kept
local to tests/benchmarks.

The default OTA path on unified CUDA-memory platforms uses CUDA-visible resource
grids and direct PDSCH device-grid mapping when available. Host, sidecar and
direct-device PDSCH grid strategies remain substitutable behind
\ref ocudu::pdsch_grid_output_strategy.

\section phy_acceleration_realtime Real-Time Safety

Acceleration code must avoid allocation and expensive setup on the slot critical
path. Factories and warmup methods own persistent pools, device handles and
preallocated workspaces. Runtime path selection must be predictable, and
fallback to the host path must preserve correctness before performance.

\section phy_acceleration_testing Testing Expectations

Changes to this area should verify:

- CUDA-enabled builds and focused accelerator E2E tests for PDSCH, PUSCH,
  PRACH, SRS, lower PHY and OFH paths.
- CUDA-disabled upper-PHY builds to prove concrete accelerator types do not leak
  into generic code.
- CPU/GPU parity for transport blocks, CRC status and scheduler-visible metrics.
- OTA validation when resource-grid or resident-buffer synchronization changes.
*/
